    {
      "id": "GENUS_LOXODONTA",
      "name": "Loxodonta",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "African elephants — the largest land animals on Earth. The African bush elephant can weigh 6,000 kg, stand 3.7 m at the shoulder, and live for 70 years. Elephants live in complex matriarchal family units led by an experienced old female whose knowledge of food and water sources, predator threats, and the ranges of other elephant groups is critical to family survival. They demonstrate tool use, mirror self-recognition, empathy, grief, and one of the most sophisticated long-distance communication systems in the animal kingdom — infrasonic rumbles below the threshold of human hearing travel through the ground up to 10 km. The African forest elephant was only formally recognised as a distinct species in 2001 through genetic analysis.",
      "lineage": "African Elephants",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "LOXODONTA_AFRICANA",
          "name": "Loxodonta africana",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "African Bush Elephant",
          "lineage": "African Elephants",
          "continents": [
            "Africa"
          ],
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "The African bush elephant (Loxodonta africana), also known as the African savanna elephant, is a species of elephant native to sub-Saharan Africa and the largest living terrestrial animal, with fully grown bulls reaching an average shoulder height of 3.04–3.36 m (10.0–11.0 ft) and a body mass of 5.2–6.9 t (5.7–7.6 short tons); the largest recorded specimen had a shoulder height of 3.96 m (13.0 ft) and an estimated body mass of 10.4 t (11.5 short tons)..",
          "sourcedFrom": "wikipedia",
          "children": [],
